---
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets'

import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/base.astro'
import RouteBar from '@/components/site/nav/route-bar.astro'
import SectionNav from '@/components/site/section-nav/section-nav.astro'
import { getLinkTargetAttributes } from '@/lib/links'
import stackrPoster from '@/assets/projects/stackr.png'
import stackrDemo from '@/assets/videos/stackr.mp4?url'

const artifactLinks = [
  {
    label: 'VS Code Marketplace',
    href: 'https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=erclx.ai-context-stacker',
  },
  {
    label: 'Open VSX',
    href: 'https://open-vsx.org/extension/erclx/ai-context-stacker',
  },
  { label: 'GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/erclx/stackr' },
]

// A `poster` attribute takes a URL rather than an image, so it reaches for the
// source file unless something derives the optimized one.
const demoPoster = await getImage({ src: stackrPoster, format: 'webp' })

const navItems = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'problem' },
  { id: 'tracks', label: 'tracks' },
  { id: 'runtime', label: 'runtime' },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Stackr · Eric Le"
  description="Named context tracks for the times a coding agent is the wrong tool. An ASCII tree, live token counts, and no runtime dependencies at all."
  surface="case-study"
>
  <SectionNav items={navItems} instant />

  <RouteBar here="Stackr" />

  <main>
    <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:pt-20 md:pb-16">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          class="text-muted-foreground text-label tracking-wide uppercase"
        >
          Project
        </p>
        <h1
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 150ms"
          class="font-display text-foreground mt-4 text-[2.25rem] leading-tight font-semibold md:text-[2.75rem]"
        >
          Stackr
        </h1>
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 250ms"
          class="text-muted-foreground text-lede mt-4"
        >
          Named context tracks for the times a coding agent is the wrong tool.
        </p>
        <ul
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 350ms"
          class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6"
        >
          {
            artifactLinks.map((link) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={link.href}
                  {...getLinkTargetAttributes(link.href)}
                  class="text-foreground text-label inline-flex min-h-11 min-w-11 items-center underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
                >
                  {link.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        {/* The demo closes the opening rather than interrupting it. It declares
            `view` so a phone, which cannot hover, plays it on screen. */}
        <figure
          data-media-host="view"
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 450ms"
          class="figure-breakout bg-card border-border/60 mt-12 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border"
        >
          <video
            src={stackrDemo}
            poster={demoPoster.src}
            muted
            loop
            playsinline
            preload="none"
            data-media-video
            aria-label="Files staged from the VS Code explorer into a named track, then copied as a directory tree followed by their contents"
            class="block h-auto w-full"></video>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="problem" class="px-6 py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl"
        >
          problem
        </h2>
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 150ms"
          class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6"
        >
          Some work is not agent work. Planning a change or arguing about
          architecture wants a chat with the right files in front of it, and
          assembling that by hand means opening each one and pasting it in
          order.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 250ms"
          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          The context is the input, and building it was the tedious part.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="tracks" class="px-6 py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl"
        >
          tracks
        </h2>
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 150ms"
          class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6"
        >
          A track is a named working set. Bug Fix #123 and Refactor Auth each
          keep their own staged list and swap on one keystroke, so the context
          for one piece of work survives while you go and do another.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 250ms"
          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          Copying a track emits an ASCII directory tree followed by the file
          contents, which gives the model the shape of the codebase before it
          reads any of it. Token counts update as the stack changes, amber above
          5,000 and red above 10,000.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="runtime" class="px-6 py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl"
        >
          runtime
        </h2>
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 150ms"
          class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6"
        >
          The extension carries no runtime dependencies. Everything runs against
          the VS Code API and the Node standard library, with esbuild bundling
          and every other package sitting in <code class="font-mono"
            >devDependencies</code
          >.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 250ms"
          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          For code loaded into someone else's editor the dependency tree is the
          risk surface, so removing it beats auditing it.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade
          data-visible="true"
          style="--fade-delay: 350ms"
          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          The other constraint is the extension host, which is shared and single
          threaded. Building an ASCII tree from a large stack would block it, so
          the tree builder works in chunks of 500 files and yields between them.
          Batch deletions use the same pattern. Neither is a general performance
          concern. Both are specific to running inside an editor you do not own.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer data-section="footer" class="border-border/60 px-6 py-10 md:py-12">
    <div class="border-border/60 mx-auto prose-column border-t pt-10">
      <a
        href="/"
        data-way-home
        class="text-foreground text-label inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-2 underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:underline md:min-h-0 md:py-2"
      >
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          width="16"
          height="16"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round"
          aria-hidden="true"
          class="text-muted-foreground"
        >
          <path d="m12 19-7-7 7-7"></path>
          <path d="M19 12H5"></path>
        </svg>
        Back to Eric Le
      </a>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script>
    import { initWayHome } from '@/components/site/case-study/way-home'
    import { initHoverVideo } from '@/lib/hover-video'

    initWayHome()
    initHoverVideo()
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
